oyment settings, storage options, and the sidecar service connection. Then I'll define the deployment mode type and a helper function to resolve which mode is active based on the environment configuration.\n\nNow I'm setting up the route registry file that imports all the necessary dependencies for database connections, storage providers, and the route definitions from across the server codebase.\n\nI'm defining a compiled route interface and building a path-to-regex converter that extracts parameter names from Express-style route patterns and converts them into regular expressions for matching incoming requests.\n\nNow I'm setting up the request resources builder, which initializes the database connection and storage service, then aggregates all the route definitions from various route modules by extracting and prefixing them appropriately. Setting up the upload handler for asset uploads, importing the necessary database schema and error utilities, then extracting the environment and database connection from the request to resolve the actor making the request.\n\nNow I'm validating the asset ID from the request parameters, querying the database to fetch the asset, and checking that it exists. Then I'm verifying the actor is authenticated and has the appropriate permissions based on their type—whether they're a board user with company access or an agent assigned to the same company. After that, I'm retrieving the request body to process the upload.\n\nWriting the upload handler... Setting up the storage request...\n\nActually, I'm noticing something about how these upload handlers work—each one independently calls `buildRequestResources(env)` for every request, which could be creating duplicate database connections. But looking at the actual flow, each request only hits one handler, so maybe this isn't the connection leak issue I thought it was.\n\nThe real problem seems to be that `buildRequestResources` creates a new postgres pool with `max: 5` but never closes it