questResources(env)` independently, which creates a new database pool each time, but since only one handler runs per request, it's not a double-connection issue — just inefficient that we're spinning up a full pool just to verify auth and fetch a row. I'll leave it as-is with the `max: 1` connection limit.\n\nFor the `node-fs.ts` dedup, I'm noticing that `notAvailableSync` and `notAvailable` are nearly identical except for their error messages.\n\nExamining the code more closely...\n\nThey're actually the same — same error message, same structure. The \"Sync\" suffix is redundant, so I can consolidate them into a single function. For `node-fs-promises.ts`, the default export is missing `lstat`, `watch`, and `pipeline` which are defined as named exports elsewhere, so I need to add those to the export object.\n\nRather than delegating this, I'll implement the changes myself since I have everything mapped out. I'm going to work through the files systematically: starting with `hyperdrive.ts`, then creating the new worker infrastructure files, rewriting the API entry point, updating the cron import, and finally fixing the shim files.\n\nFor the environment configuration, I'll define the `Env` interface without importing the runtime `R2Provider` class. The route registry will use a module-level function for the `ext` helper, and the upload handlers will export a function that registers routes onto the Hono app instance. After that I need to add the configuration files for the Cloudflare package, update the wrangler compatibility date, and test the build.\n\nI'm noticing that the upload handlers call `buildRequestResources` but only use the `db` from it—the compiled routes and storage aren't needed. I could extract a simpler `createRequestDb` function to avoid building all 229 route handlers unnecessarily, but that's beyond the scope of this refactor. I'll stick with using the existing function as-is.",
